# 9. ACCESSIBILITY REQUIREMENTS

The implementation should target WCAG 2.2 Level AA.

## 9.1 Keyboard accessibility

- All interactive controls must be keyboard accessible.
- Focus indicators must be visible.
- Focus order must be logical.
- Modal focus must be managed correctly.
- No keyboard traps.
- Escape behavior must be appropriate for dismissible overlays.

## 9.2 Screen readers

- Use semantic HTML.
- Provide accessible labels for icon-only controls.
- Use appropriate heading hierarchy.
- Associate validation errors with their fields.
- Announce important asynchronous status changes appropriately.
- Do not communicate state through color alone.

## 9.3 Color and contrast

- Maintain sufficient contrast for text and controls.
- Support visible focus states.
- Ensure disabled and selected states remain understandable.
- Avoid color-only success and error indicators.

## 9.4 Touch accessibility

- Interactive controls should generally provide at least a 44 × 44 CSS pixel touch area where practical.
- Provide adequate spacing between adjacent actions.
- Avoid tiny reaction, menu, and navigation controls.

## 9.5 Motion

- Avoid unnecessary motion.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.
- Do not use animations that obstruct interaction.
- Avoid motion that can trigger discomfort.

## 9.6 Forms

- Use persistent field labels.
- Do not rely solely on placeholder text.
- Clearly identify required fields.
- Provide understandable error descriptions.
- Maintain appropriate focus after validation failures.

